  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/186ac941080da34b7c149534a2e255852241b28855c0ef15db2beee2d692007a.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,3]$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,5)$\n- $x\\in (4,6)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,3].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,3),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,5)$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(4,6).$ It is continuous on $[3,\\infty )$ and this contains the interval $(4,6).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
